This code uses NLTK to tokenize the text into words, stem the words, and compute the cosine similarity between the main theme in Romeo and Juliet and other works of Shakespeare. The similarity is a value between 0 and 1, where 1 means the two texts are identical in terms of the main theme. The code generates a comparison report that shows the similarity between Romeo and Juliet and other works of Shakespeare based on the main theme.

To calculate the angle between two vectors using MATLAB, you can use the dot product formula:
cos(theta) = (A . B) / (|A| * |B|)
where A and B are the two vectors, |A| and |B| are the magnitudes of the vectors, and theta is the angle between them.
Here's the MATLAB code to calculate the angle between the given vectors:
```matlab
% Define the vectors
A = [4 2];
B = [-3 5];
% Calculate the dot product
dotProduct = dot(A,B);
% Calculate the magnitudes of the vectors
magA = norm(A);
magB = norm(B);
% Calculate the cosine of the angle between the vectors
cosTheta = dotProduct / (magA * magB);
% Calculate the angle in radians and degrees
thetaRad = acos(cosTheta);
thetaDeg = rad2deg(thetaRad);
% Display the angle in degrees
disp(['The angle between the vectors is ', num2str(thetaDeg), ' degrees.']);
```
When you run this code, the output should be:
```
The angle between the vectors is 123.69 degrees.
```
So the angle between the vectors A and B is approximately 123.69 degrees.

