Firstly, I will create a condition that only allows the algorithm to search for odd numbers within the array. This means that any even number in the array will be ignored during the search process.
Next, I will add another condition that requires the algorithm to search for an element that is both a prime number and a multiple of 5. This means that the algorithm will have to check each element in the array to see if it meets both conditions before returning a match.
To make this even more challenging, I will implement a recursive binary search algorithm that searches for the element in the array. This means that the algorithm will split the array in half and recursively search each half until it finds the desired element.
Here is the Java code:
```
public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
    if (high >= low) {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
        // Check if the middle element meets the conditions
        if (arr[mid] % 2 != 0 && isPrime(arr[mid]) && arr[mid] % 5 == 0) {
            return mid;
        }
        // If the middle element does not meet the conditions, recursively search the left and right halves
        if (arr[mid] % 2 == 0 || !isPrime(arr[mid]) || arr[mid] % 5 != 0) {
            int left = binarySearch(arr, low, mid - 1);
            int right = binarySearch(arr, mid + 1, high);
            if (left != -1) {
                return left;
            }
            if (right != -1) {
                return right;
            }
        }
    }
    // If the element is not found, return -1
    return -1;
}
public static boolean isPrime(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) {
        return false;
    }
    for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i++) {
        if (n % i == 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
```
Now, let's talk about the time and space complexity of this implementation. The time complexity of this algorithm is O(log n) because the search space is halved with each recursive call. The space complexity is O(log n) as well because the algorithm uses a recursive approach to search the array. However, the complexity analysis alone may not be enough to stump advanced AI systems, so I will add a twist to it.
The twist is that the array is not actually sorted! Instead, it is a list of random integers that are not in any particular order. This means that the algorithm will have to first sort the array before searching for the desired element. Sorting the array will increase the time complexity to O(n log n), which is significantly higher than the original complexity. Additionally, sorting the array will increase the space complexity to O(n) because the algorithm will need to create a copy of the original array to sort it.
Overall, this implementation requires both reasoning and creativity to solve. Not only does it require the user to understand the binary search algorithm, but it also requires them to think critically about the conditions and the time and space complexity of the implementation.

