To enhance the accuracy of car price prediction based on age and income variables, we can incorporate the following advanced techniques into linear regression implementation using PHP:
1. Regularization: Regularization is a technique used to prevent overfitting in a linear regression model. It involves adding a penalty term to the cost function that shrinks the coefficients towards zero. This helps to reduce the complexity of the model and improve its generalizability. In PHP, we can implement regularization using the L1 or L2 norm.
2. Feature scaling: Feature scaling is a technique used to standardize the range of input variables. This helps to prevent numerical instability and improve the convergence of the optimization algorithm. In PHP, we can implement feature scaling using the standardization or normalization method.
3. Cross-validation: Cross-validation is a technique used to assess the generalizability of a model. It involves splitting the data into training and testing sets multiple times and evaluating the performance of the model on each set. In PHP, we can implement cross-validation using the k-fold method.
Here is a comprehensive and detailed PHP code that incorporates these techniques:
```php
<?php
// Load the dataset
$data = array(
    array(25, 50000, 25000),
    array(30, 60000, 30000),
    array(35, 70000, 35000),
    array(40, 80000, 40000),
    array(45, 90000, 45000),
    array(50, 100000, 50000),
    array(55, 110000, 55000),
    array(60, 120000, 60000),
    array(65, 130000, 65000),
    array(70, 140000, 70000)
);
// Split the data into input (X) and output (y) variables
$X = array();
$y = array();
foreach ($data as $row) {
    $X[] = array($row[0], $row[1]);
    $y[] = $row[2];
}
// Implement feature scaling using standardization method
function standardize($X) {
    $means = array();
    $stds = array();
    for ($i = 0; $i < count($X[0]); $i++) {
        $col = array_column($X, $i);
        $mean = array_sum($col) / count($col);
        $std = sqrt(array_sum(array_map(function($x) use ($mean) { return pow($x - $mean, 2); }, $col)) / (count($col) - 1));
        $means[] = $mean;
        $stds[] = $std;
    }
    $X_std = array();
    foreach ($X as $row) {
        $X_std_row = array();
        for ($i = 0; $i < count($row); $i++) {
            $X_std_row[] = ($row[$i] - $means[$i]) / $stds[$i];
        }
        $X_std[] = $X_std_row;
    }
    return array($X_std, $means, $stds);
}
list($X_std, $means, $stds) = standardize($X);
// Implement regularization using L2 norm
function l2_regularization($theta, $lambda) {
    $theta_reg = array();
    for ($i = 1; $i < count($theta); $i++) {
        $theta_reg[] = $theta[$i];
    }
    return ($lambda / 2) * array_sum(array_map(function($x) { return pow($x, 2); }, $theta_reg));
}
// Implement the cost function
function cost_function($theta, $X, $y, $lambda) {
    $m = count($y);
    $J = 0;
    for ($i = 0; $i < $m; $i++) {
        $hypothesis = $theta[0] + ($theta[1] * $X[$i][0]) + ($theta[2] * $X[$i][1]);
        $J += pow(($hypothesis - $y[$i]), 2);
    }
    $J /= (2 * $m);
    $J += l2_regularization($theta, $lambda) / $m;
    return $J;
}
// Implement the gradient descent algorithm
function gradient_descent($theta, $X, $y, $alpha, $lambda, $iterations) {
    $m = count($y);
    $J_history = array();
    for ($iter = 1; $iter <= $iterations; $iter++) {
        $theta_new = array();
        $theta_new[] = $theta[0] - ($alpha / $m) * array_sum(array_map(function($x, $y, $theta) { return ($theta[0] + ($theta[1] * $x[0]) + ($theta[2] * $x[1]) - $y); }, $X, $y, $theta));
        for ($j = 1; $j < count($theta); $j++) {
            $theta_new[] = $theta[$j] * (1 - ($alpha * $lambda / $m)) - ($alpha / $m) * array_sum(array_map(function($x, $y, $theta, $j) { return (($theta[0] + ($theta[1] * $x[0]) + ($theta[2] * $x[1]) - $y) * $x[$j]); }, $X, $y, $theta, $j));
        }
        $theta = $theta_new;
        $J_history[] = cost_function($theta, $X, $y, $lambda);
    }
    return array($theta, $J_history);
}
// Implement cross-validation using k-fold method
function k_fold_cross_validation($X, $y, $k, $alpha, $lambda, $iterations) {
    $m = count($y);
    $fold_size = floor($m / $k);
    $J_history = array();
    for ($i = 0; $i < $k; $i++) {
        $X_train = $X_test = $y_train = $y_test = array();
        for ($j = 0; $j < $m; $j++) {
            if ($j >= $i * $fold_size && $j < ($i + 1) * $fold_size) {
                $X_test[] = $X[$j];
                $y_test[] = $y[$j];
            } else {
                $X_train[] = $X[$j];
                $y_train[] = $y[$j];
            }
        }
        list($X_train_std, $means, $stds) = standardize($X_train);
        $theta = array(0, 0, 0);
        list($theta, $J_history_fold) = gradient_descent($theta, $X_train_std, $y_train, $alpha, $lambda, $iterations);
        $X_test_std = array();
        foreach ($X_test as $row) {
            $X_test_std_row = array();
            for ($j = 0; $j < count($row); $j++) {
                $X_test_std_row[] = ($row[$j] - $means[$j]) / $stds[$j];
            }
            $X_test_std[] = $X_test_std_row;
        }
        $J_history_fold[] = cost_function($theta, $X_test_std, $y_test, $lambda);
        $J_history[] = $J_history_fold;
    }
    return $J_history;
}
// Set the hyperparameters
$alpha = 0.01;
$lambda = 0.1;
$iterations = 1000;
$k = 5;
// Run the cross-validation
$J_history = k_fold_cross_validation($X, $y, $k, $alpha, $lambda, $iterations);
// Calculate the mean and standard deviation of the cost function
$J_mean = array_map(function($x) { return array_sum($x) / count($x); }, $J_history);
$J_std = array_map(function($x, $mean) { return sqrt(array_sum(array_map(function($x, $mean) { return pow($x - $mean, 2); }, $x, array_fill(0, count($x), $mean))) / (count($x) - 1)); }, $J_history, $J_mean);
// Print the results
echo "Mean of cost function: " . array_sum($J_mean) / count($J_mean) . "\n";
echo "Standard deviation of cost function: " . array_sum($J_std) / count($J_std) . "\n";
?>
```
In this code, we first load the dataset and split it into input (X) and output (y) variables. We then implement feature scaling using the standardization method and regularization using the L2 norm. We also implement the cost function and gradient descent algorithm. Finally, we implement cross-validation using the k-fold method and calculate the mean and standard deviation of the cost function. The output of the code is the mean and standard deviation of the cost function over the k folds.

