```go
package main
import (
	"fmt"
	"math"
)
func isPrime(n int) bool {
	if n <= 1 {
		return false
	}
	if n <= 3 {
		return true
	}
	if n%2 == 0 || n%3 == 0 {
		return false
	}
	for i := 5; i <= int(math.Sqrt(float64(n))); i += 6 {
		if n%i == 0 || n%(i+2) == 0 {
			return false
		}
	}
	return true
}
func main() {
	fmt.Println(isPrime(11)) // true
	fmt.Println(isPrime(15)) // false
}
```
The program first checks if the input number is less than or equal to 1, in which case it returns false as 1 and numbers less than 1 are not considered prime. If the number is less than or equal to 3, it returns true as 2 and 3 are prime numbers.
Next, the program checks if the number is divisible by 2 or 3, in which case it returns false as even numbers and numbers divisible by 3 are not prime.
Finally, the program checks for divisibility by all other prime numbers up to the square root of the input number, skipping multiples of 2 and 3. If the number is divisible by any of these prime numbers, it returns false. Otherwise, it returns true.
This program is efficient because it only checks for divisibility by prime numbers, and it skips multiples of 2 and 3, which are not prime. This reduces the number of checks required and makes the program faster.

